# PR delivery timing

Actions merges ready PRs without waiting for CI (`github-delivery.mdc` item
10; AGENTS.md §5). That policy is about **checks**: a slow test suite never
gates delivery, and findings of any kind stay advisory. This rule is about
**timing**, and it exists because of one session in which six of nine merged
PRs were repairs to bugs an automated review had reported minutes *after* the
auto-merge landed. Each fix needed a whole follow-up PR instead of a push to
the branch that had been open seconds earlier.

## The two gates

Both are in `decide()` in `tools/pr_delivery.py` -- the one function every
merge path calls: the CI `Auto-merge` job (`merge --pr`), the scheduled and
CI-completion sweep (`sweep`), and a manual `workflow_dispatch`.

1. **Age floor -- per head.** A head younger than `MIN_PR_AGE_SECONDS` (five
   minutes, in `tools/pr_review_status.py`) returns `WAIT` / `settling`. The
   age is the newest of the PR's opening and its latest commit, so **a push
   restarts the floor**: measured from `createdAt` alone it protected only
   the first commit, and any later push was eligible at once. Reviews took
   two to four minutes on every PR measured; the floor gives one time to
   exist. It needs no reviewer to be present at all, which is why it closes
   the race that comment-based detection cannot.
   Known gap: the commit's `committedDate` is not its push time. A commit
   made an hour ago and pushed now reads as old. Agents that commit and push
   in one step -- Nova's normal flow -- are covered; a long-parked local
   commit is not.
2. **In-flight review.** A review still running against the *current head*
   returns `WAIT` / `review_running`. This is the backstop for a review slower
   than the floor. The signal is the reviewer's summary comment -- untrusted
   bot markdown, matched and never executed -- and anything unrecognised
   reads as "not running", so a reviewer that changes format or disappears
   can never wedge delivery shut.

Neither gate reads what a review concluded. `WAIT`, never `BLOCK`: a later
sweep merges it, and CI completing fires one.

**The merge is bound to the head that passed the gates.** `decide()` is pure
over a fetched snapshot, so the write must carry that snapshot's identity or
the two can name different heads: the merge payload sends `sha:
pr.headRefOid`, and GitHub answers `409` when the head has moved since. A
`409` is not a failure -- `cmd_merge` loops back to `_fetch_pr` and re-decides
(the new head reads `settling`), and `cmd_sweep` prints `#N skip head_moved`
and carries on. Without it a `synchronize` push landing between the last fetch
and the `PUT` ships a head that passed neither the floor nor the in-flight
review check, and the push's own `Auto-merge` run then finds the PR closed
(`skip not_open`), so nothing evaluates it afterwards either.

**The merge job waits the floor out.** Nothing fires when the floor expires,
so `cmd_merge` sleeps through the remaining settling time and re-decides,
instead of leaving a PR whose CI finished early to the hourly cron -- up to
an hour for a gate meant to cost five minutes. The wait is capped at twice
the floor, because a head that keeps moving keeps resetting the floor and
must not pin a runner. The `Auto-merge` job's `timeout-minutes` in
`deploy.yml` has to exceed that cap plus setup, or the job is killed
mid-wait and reads as a red check. The sweep does not wait; it is periodic.

## Why the gates are in `decide()` and nowhere else

The first version put a grace period in `cmd_merge` only. Within the hour the
sweep -- which never called it -- merged #410 one second after the reviewer
announced, while the merge job was still correctly waiting (`#410 skip
not_open` in its log). A gate that lives in one caller is a gate one caller
has. Put timing logic in `decide()`, or it is not delivery policy, it is a
property of whichever path happened to be edited.

## Escape hatches

- `--min-age-seconds 0` on `merge` or `sweep` restores instant merge.
- `do-not-merge` label or draft holds a PR regardless, as before.

## Enforcement

`tools/test_pr_review_status.py` pins: the floor holds a fresh PR on **both**
`cmd_merge` and `cmd_sweep`; a push restarts it; an old PR with an old head
merges; missing commit data falls back to the PR's age; a settled PR still
waits on a running review; the merge job waits the floor out and merges in
the same run, and that wait is capped when the head keeps moving; a held PR
never waits; an unknown age never holds; a zero floor restores instant merge;
the PR query asks for `createdAt` and `commits`. A change to merge timing
changes those tests first.

